You want to send a picture of the neighborhood noticeboard and a PDF
of the event program. The picture looks ordinary in your photo app.
The PDF looks ready to print. Before sharing, you want to answer three
questions: are these files what their names say they are, do they
include anything private, and will the copies actually work for the
person receiving them? Those are three separate checks.

\begin{objectives}
\item Identify an unfamiliar file without trusting its suffix alone.
\item Inspect selected document and media properties with local tools.
\item Convert a synthetic file into a new directory and check the result.
\end{objectives}

\section{A filename is a clue, not a guarantee}
\index{file@\texttt{file}}\index{file formats}\index{metadata}
A suffix such as \texttt{.png} or \texttt{.pdf} helps applications
decide what to open. Renaming a file from \texttt{.txt} to \texttt{.pdf}
doesn't rewrite any bytes. \cmd|file| looks at properties such as
recognizable byte patterns and text encodings to make an educated
guess. This is a useful first observation, not a security scan.
A truncated, mislabeled, unfamiliar, or specially constructed file
can confuse a tool. ``Data'' often means that \cmd|file| could not
classify the bytes more precisely, not that the file is harmless.
An unexpected attachment should not be opened just because its result
looks ordinary. Ask for a known valid copy through a trusted route.
Figure~\ref{fig:42-checks} separates recognition from inspection.

All optional tooling below runs locally on files you make. No images,
maps, or documents go to a remote service. On Linux, macOS, FreeBSD,
OpenBSD and NetBSD, check the installed manual for \cmd|file| and
the packages offered for the optional examples. An exact classification
depends on the program and its database. See Chapter~\ref{ch:35}
before installing software. Do not install something from an
attachment's instructions just to be able to inspect the attachment.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-42/three-checks.tikz}
\caption{Classification, inspection, and opening answer different questions.}\label{fig:42-checks}
\VisualDescription{A suffix offers a hint, a classifier tries to recognize the bytes, and a format-aware tool examines the recognized contents.}
\end{figure}

Use a fresh temporary directory and a shell with \texttt{sh}
syntax. \texttt{-d} requests a new directory; \cmd|cd| changes
the working directory. \texttt{mktemp} is common but not
specified by POSIX; this example asks it to replace the
final \texttt{X}s with a unique suffix in the trusted system
temporary directory. Use another trusted absolute temporary
path if needed. Each \texttt{\&\&} requires the previous
command to succeed. \texttt{pwd -P} resolves symbolic links
in the directory path. The setup clears any old value, then
records and prints the exact physical path once the new
directory is ready. If it prints no path, stop.

\begin{terminal}
unset medialab
newlab=$(mktemp -d /tmp/utp-media.XXXXXXXX) &&
  cd "$newlab" &&
  medialab=$(pwd -P) &&
  printf '%s\n' "$medialab"
\end{terminal}

Define this check in the same shell. The \texttt{-n} test
requires a nonempty saved path, and the equality test compares
the entire current physical path to it. An empty value, a
different parent directory, or a similar-looking name isn't a
match. A failed check prints a diagnostic on standard error
and returns status one; the following command does not run.
If it fails, don't change the saved value to make it pass.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab() {
  if test -n "$medialab" && test "$(pwd -P)" = "$medialab"; then
    return 0
  fi
  printf '%s\n' 'Stop: not in the original media lab.' >&2
  return 1
}
in_media_lab && mkdir release
\end{terminal}

Only in this confirmed fresh lab, make the first fixture. The
\texttt{>} redirection below can overwrite a file; it's safe
here because the directory is new and the paths are just fixtures.
The \texttt{release} directory will hold derivative files
only. A derivative is a new copy made from the original, not the
master you want to keep.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab &&
  printf '%s\n' 'This is only a line of text.' > ./not-a-pdf.pdf &&
  file ./not-a-pdf.pdf
\end{terminal}

Expect a text description rather than a PDF classification.
The \texttt{./} prefix gives an explicit local path, so even a
name that begins with a hyphen won't be mistaken for an option.
This is not a damaged PDF; you know the exact line you wrote.
You can compare this exercise with inspecting a real PDF later.
If you received a file like this from a colleague, the next step
would be asking how it was created, not renaming it again.

\section{Inspect a practice image and make a derivative}\label{sec:42-image}
\index{magick@\texttt{magick}}\index{image conversion}
Create a tiny plain PPM image. PPM is a simple picture format
in which you can see the values that make the pixels. \texttt{P3}
is the signature of a plain-text PPM, \texttt{2 2} is width and
height in pixels, and \texttt{255} is the maximum sample value
in this file. Each group of three numbers is red, green, and
blue. The first row contains red and green pixels; the second
contains blue and white pixels. \cmd|printf| prints the six
arguments as separate lines (including the comment beginning with
\texttt{\#}); real pictures need not be this simple, and files
from your camera will probably be compressed binary formats.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab &&
  printf '%s\n' P3 '# synthetic tiles' '2 2' '255' \
    '255 0 0  0 255 0' '0 0 255  255 255 255' > ./tiles.ppm &&
  file ./tiles.ppm
\end{terminal}

\cmd|file| may recognize the image as Netpbm or portable pixmap
data and report its dimensions. To convert it, this example uses
ImageMagick 7, whose main command is \cmd|magick|. Older ImageMagick
6 packages have different command forms; don't substitute a command
named \texttt{convert} from another vendor. Check the version
before calling these commands. \cmd|command -v| locates the name
using the shell's own lookup rules; \texttt{-version} asks
ImageMagick to print its version information and exits.

\begin{terminal}
command -v magick
magick -version
\end{terminal}

If the tool is unavailable, the PPM and the text comparison are
enough for the format-identification exercise. Otherwise use a
new output file. The first operand is the input; \texttt{-set
comment} sets a property on the output image, whose format is
chosen from the \texttt{.png} suffix. The comment is invented
so we have a harmless example of information that isn't part of
the pixels. ImageMagick can replace an existing output file;
check that \texttt{./tiles.png} does not exist before running it.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab &&
  magick ./tiles.ppm -set comment 'fictional noticeboard' ./tiles.png &&
  file ./tiles.png
\end{terminal}

ImageMagick's \texttt{identify} subcommand reads information
about the image. \texttt{-format} takes the requested display
format: \texttt{\%w} and \texttt{\%h} ask for width and height,
\texttt{\%c} asks for the comment and \texttt{\textbackslash n}
ends the line. Keep the format in quotes so the shell passes it
as one argument.

\begin{terminal}
magick identify -format '%w x %h: %c\n' ./tiles.png
\end{terminal}

Expect \texttt{2 x 2: fictional noticeboard}. If you don't
see the comment, check the command, output type and local
version before claiming anything about what was retained.
Now make a separate, new release copy. \texttt{-strip} removes
profiles and comments in the ImageMagick conversion; don't
treat it as a universal eraser for every possible file format.
Apply the same reporting command to both paths to see whether
this particular field changed. Don't write the result over the
only original. If a destination already exists, inspect it
and choose a different unused name.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab &&
  magick ./tiles.png -strip ./release/tiles-share.png &&
  magick identify -format '%w x %h: %c\n' ./tiles.png &&
  magick identify -format '%w x %h: %c\n' ./release/tiles-share.png
\end{terminal}

The lab's release copy should still be two by two, with no
comment shown by this inspection; the original still has its
comment. Verify the view too. Open the synthetic derivative in
your normal local viewer and zoom in if necessary, or read its
pixels below. Removing profiles from a real photo can also change
how colors display. Some image tools use an orientation tag to
rotate a picture in the viewer; stripping the tag without first
applying the orientation can leave the copy sideways. Compare the
rendered original and release copy and choose a tool and export
process that fit the actual image.
Figure~\ref{fig:42-derivative} puts the independent inspection after
the conversion, with the original untouched.

Choose a target format for the recipient's task. A document for
printing and a small picture for a chat window may have very
different requirements. Pixel dimensions give you a count, not a
promise that a small image will be legible when enlarged. PNG can
preserve a graphical original without the kind of change in
pixel values often introduced by a lossy photographic format.
Formats can also differ in whether they support transparency,
multiple frames, or print color information. Test the single
derivative you need; don't run a conversion across an entire
photo folder merely to see whether the words ``PNG image''
appear in the output.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-42/derivative.tikz}
\caption{Keep the master and inspect the sharing copy separately.}\label{fig:42-derivative}
\VisualDescription{The original goes into a conversion, but remains unchanged. A new sharing copy goes through a separate inspection.}
\end{figure}

There are several paths for information to leave your machine:
embedded metadata, visible content, companion files, and the
names and paths of the files you send. A camera can record
location in metadata. A badge reflected in a window can disclose
a name directly in the pixels. A shortened image can travel with
a sidecar file that describes edits or contains notes. Neither
stripping a comment nor giving the picture a blander filename
addresses all of these. Inspect the image you will actually share
and stage only intended files. If anything must be formally
redacted, use an approved workflow and verify that the underlying
content is gone, not merely covered by a drawing.

For example, a photo taken for a public article can be
appropriate to share with its original dimensions and colors but
not with an embedded street coordinate. A family photo can have
no embedded location at all and still make its location obvious
from a readable house number. For this lab the invented comment
has no consequences: one field provides a simple observable
change. In a real workflow, the decision about what to keep takes
longer than the command. Agree with the sender or subject about
what may be published, keep the original in the approved place,
and document the crop, export, or removal you actually performed.
Don't post the original and the redacted derivative together.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.35in}Y}
\toprule
Where to look & Typical examples and what to check \\
\midrule
Visible image or page & Names on signs, reflections, handwriting, screen contents and background details. Inspect the rendered copy. \\
Embedded properties & Creator names, timestamps, location, color profiles, comments. Inspect with a format-aware tool; verify any specific field you intended to remove. \\
Content not visible by default & PDF annotations, attachments or underlying text; media can also have embedded data or multiple tracks. \\
Filename and neighbors & Project names, personal initials, companion files and unintentional exports. Inspect the set of files to be shared. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Read a document without treating one report as complete}
\index{pdfinfo@\texttt{pdfinfo}}\index{pdftotext@\texttt{pdftotext}}
Poppler's \cmd|pdfinfo| summarizes information about a PDF.
Look for page count, page size, encryption information and
any creator or title fields. Empty metadata fields don't
establish that there's no identifying material in the page
content or in other PDF objects. \cmd|pdftotext| extracts
accessible text; it needn't capture visual ordering, tables, or
text in a scanned image. In a properly authorized case, optical
character recognition is another step, not evidence that a
blank \cmd|pdftotext| result meant an empty page. A report is a
tool for asking the next question, not proof of total absence.

For an optional hands-on comparison, create a synthetic PDF
if \cmd|pdflatex| is already installed, or use a trusted blank
test document you made yourself. The portable shell command
below writes three lines of LaTeX source. Single quotes pass
backslashes and braces literally. The TeX engine composes that
text into a one-page PDF. \texttt{-interaction=nonstopmode}
prevents interactive questions; \texttt{-halt-on-error} tells
it to stop on an error. Check its result before inspecting the
output. Use the exact fixture only, not LaTeX source from
an unknown sender. No document in this chapter needs shell escape.

\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab &&
  printf '%s\n' '\documentclass{article}' '\begin{document}' \
    'The practice event begins at noon.\end{document}' > ./program.tex &&
  pdflatex -no-shell-escape -interaction=nonstopmode \
    -halt-on-error ./program.tex
\end{terminal}

The explicit \texttt{-no-shell-escape} option tells the engine
not to run external shell commands. This produces a PDF and
auxiliary \texttt{.aux} and \texttt{.log} files in the practice
directory; they are not part of the sharing copy. If Poppler's
tools are installed, use the following. The \texttt{-} output
operand tells \cmd|pdftotext| to print on standard output.
Expected text includes the line about noon and may include a
page number. If \cmd|pdfinfo| says one page and \cmd|pdftotext|
returns an empty result, report those observations separately
instead of declaring that the file lacks content.

Consider a draft handout saved as a series of scanned pages. It
can appear to be an ordinary spacious text document and yet only
contain photographs of text to a basic extractor. A different PDF
might display a black rectangle placed on top of readable
underlying text; a text extractor may still read through it.
Neither result proves more than the method used to obtain it.
For a friendly review, report which pages you opened and which
kind of text the extractor found. Don't publish the extracted
text if it contains the very names you wanted to check for.
When accessibility or privacy matters, use the release procedure
required for those needs rather than trying to deduce compliance
from ``one page'' and a handful of metadata fields.

Some readers need a tagged PDF with a useful reading order, or a
plain-text companion to a graphic handout. A text extraction is
helpful for looking for missing headings and broken column order,
but it isn't a substitute for trying the actual released document
with the means people will use to read it. If a converted image
looks fine but the label is now too small to read, the problem
is not resolved by confirming it matches the expected file type.

\begin{terminal}
pdfinfo ./program.pdf
pdftotext ./program.pdf -
\end{terminal}

\section{Pick the tool that speaks the format}
A PDF page is not a stream of audio samples. For media files,
\cmd|ffprobe|, distributed with FFmpeg, reports on the container
(the wrapper format) and the streams (video, audio or other
tracks in the wrapper). For a known local file \texttt{clip.wav},
the following selects information about streams and asks for
errors only in the logging stream. This is a command form only;
the image lab hasn't created that file.

\begin{terminal}[title={Illustrative form for a trusted local audio fixture}]
ffprobe -v error -show_streams ./clip.wav
\end{terminal}

\texttt{-v error} limits logging; it does not suppress the
requested information. \texttt{-show\_streams} requests one
section per stream found. For an ordinary WAV file expect
an audio section; \texttt{codec\_name} describes its encoding
and \texttt{channels} the number of audio channels. A different
container might include separate video and audio streams.
These properties don't tell you whether a voice can be
recognized or whether the clip includes a private conversation.
Review content as well as reported fields. Some media tools
accept network addresses as inputs, but this chapter uses
explicit local paths, never credentials, URLs, or downloaded
conversion services.

Make a habit of writing down what preservation means before a
conversion. For a text handout it might mean the number of
pages, the words, and legibility at ordinary zoom. For a recording
it might include its complete duration, audible speech, synchronization
between picture and sound, and the intended caption track. Two
container files can be different byte for byte while presenting
the same picture or recording. Conversely, a successful conversion
can discard a caption track you didn't know the original had.
Chapter~\ref{ch:41} keeps the master recoverable. This chapter
keeps the derivative fit for a specific use; the two checks belong
together.

After inspecting the copy, recheck where it will go. A derivative
stored in a directory named \texttt{release} is ready for further
review, not automatically approved for distribution. Some tools
add build dates or software version information during conversion.
Read the actual output again if you repeat the export using a
different preset, or if someone edits the exported file. Use the
transfer checks from Chapter~\ref{ch:39} for the exact set you
intend to hand off, rather than pointing a sharing tool at your
entire work area.
Figure~\ref{fig:42-content} names three channels to check in any
sharing copy, even one you didn't have to convert.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.3in}Y}
\toprule
Question & Tool and limit \\
\midrule
What format is this probably? & \texttt{file}: guess based on file properties; doesn't validate the whole file. \\
How many pages and what size? & Poppler \texttt{pdfinfo}: PDF summary, not a full review of embedded content. \\
What text is extractable? & Poppler \texttt{pdftotext}: may miss scans and doesn't preserve every aspect of layout. \\
What tracks are present? & FFmpeg \texttt{ffprobe}: properties of streams \emph{as recognized by the tool}; not a listening or viewing check. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering\input{figures/chapter-42/content.tikz}
\caption{Some information is rendered, while other information travels with it.}\label{fig:42-content}
\VisualDescription{A sharing copy can carry visible content, hidden or embedded information, and a revealing filename. Check all three before delivery.}
\end{figure}

\begin{dialoguebox}{The clean picture}
\Brian{The comment is gone. Can I send the picture now?}
\Stephen{That copy no longer reports the comment in our check.}
\Brian{But the address is still painted on the front door in the picture.}
\Stephen{Right. Removing a property didn't remove anything from the scene.}
\Brian{And if I crop the address away, the filename could still be the street name.}
\Stephen{Exactly. Inspect the picture, properties and filename you're actually releasing.}
\Brian{So the command proved one thing, not permission to share.}
\Stephen{That's the distinction.}
\DialogueConclusion{Check the copy you will actually share.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\begin{exercise}{Distinguish a suffix from a format}\label{ex:42-suffix}
Compare the results of \texttt{file} on the pretend PDF
and on the PPM. If you made the one-page program, include
that file too. Identify which results were recognizable by
their contents. This creates no more files.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|file ./not-a-pdf.pdf ./tiles.ppm| should identify text
and a PPM or Netpbm picture, not two things based only on
suffixes. \cmd|file ./program.pdf|, if created successfully,
should identify a PDF. A recognized format still does not tell
you whether it's suitable to share.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Check a derivative's pixels and comment}\label{ex:42-derivative}
If you have ImageMagick 7, inspect the dimensions and comment
of the PNG pair. View the release image, or print its pixels
using the solution below. If you don't have the tool, read the
PPM triples and state the intended pixel layout. Keep the
fixtures for cleanup.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use the earlier \cmd|identify| form to see that both are
two by two, with the comment absent from the release copy.
\cmd|magick ./release/tiles-share.png txt:-| uses the special
\texttt{txt:} output format for a textual pixel enumeration;
the hyphen sends it to standard output. Check for red, green,
blue, and white in the intended positions, not exact spelling
of color names. Neither dimensions nor comment alone tells
you what the pixels show.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Review what would leave the lab}\label{ex:42-review}
List the release directory and choose which file, if any, would
be appropriate to send for this make-believe task. Explain why
the working directory should not be sent as a bundle. Don't send
anything. Finish by removing only your fixtures.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\cmd|ls -a ./release| should show only the derivative and the
ordinary dot entries if conversion was performed. The master,
pretend PDF, and optional TeX build files are unrelated to that
image handoff. Follow the lab cleanup steps after this review.
\end{solution}

\paragraph{Lab cleanup.}
After finishing the exercises, use the same exact-path check
before removing any files. The braces group the whole cleanup
so none of it runs when the check fails. Skip nonexistent
optional outputs if typing the commands individually, but
keep the path check before every removal.
\cmd|rm -i| asks for confirmation; confirm only the synthetic
fixtures. If a command failed part way through the lab, inspect
which outputs it actually created before removing them.
\begin{terminal}
in_media_lab && {
  rm -i ./not-a-pdf.pdf ./tiles.ppm
  rm -i ./tiles.png ./release/tiles-share.png
  rm -i ./program.tex ./program.pdf ./program.aux ./program.log
  rmdir ./release
  cd && rmdir "$medialab"
}
\end{terminal}
If \cmd|rmdir| refuses, inspect the directory for unexpected
files rather than deleting everything recursively.
\begin{takeaways}
\item Check classification from contents, then use a tool that understands the format.
\item Preserve the original and write conversion output somewhere new.
\item Inspect the delivered copy, including what you can see and what it carries silently.
\end{takeaways}

